44
00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:53.769
ignacio: potential block hash of code executions within the block use the correct data.

45
00:21:55.180 --> 00:22:05.899
ignacio: And what DCIP is trying to do is to say, okay, now BlockHash, needs to use, EIP2935 system contract.

46
00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:12.489
ignacio: To really access the hashes. So basically, this means that this execution witness

47
00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:24.600
ignacio: container can really remove these headers, and this information is indirectly in this state field, right? Because BlueHash will be simply accessing the state tree.

48
00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:27.680
ignacio: And this is indirectly captured by state.

49
00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:33.439
ignacio: So we can remove this, this simplifies the execution with next generation, this simplifies

50
00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:38.220
ignacio: A lot of test cases that we have today, the 2025.

51
00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:39.860
ignacio: regarding, like.

52
00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:48.670
ignacio: Receiving an invalid list of headers with a gap in the middle, with the chain. the… the… the… Parent.

53
00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:54.570
ignacio: Hash, not really matching the…

54
00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:57.850
ignacio: Correctly, and a lot of things that can go wrong.

55
00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:10.789
ignacio: So yeah, this will simplify this field, and hopefully in the future, whenever PVT comes, and bytecode is chunkified, this code still will also go away.

56
00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:14.380
ignacio: Because code will be part of the state.

57
00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:18.789
ignacio: And the execution with this would be mainly.

58
00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:23.630
ignacio: just… state, like, tree nodes.

59
00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:29.980
ignacio: So that's kind of the long-term direction that we are going, and 7709 is just

60
00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:34.749
